idea:
Add one learned detector for mild or partial damage on top of the existing damage filter. It is tuned to drop about 2% of clean docs in total, unlike experiment 4, which dropped at least 5% per test.

1. Synthetic negatives. For each of the 200 target docs, make seeded damaged copies of the first-400-token window at graded intensities:
   a. Local word shuffles: permute words inside consecutive blocks of 3, 5 and 10 tokens.
   b. Partial shuffles: pick 15%, 30% and 60% of token positions and permute the tokens among those positions.
   c. Character substitutions at 2%, 5% and 10%.
   d. Random word deletion at 20%.
   e. Random adjacent-word duplication at 15%.
   That gives about 11 negatives per target doc.

2. Features per window. Reuse the existing leave-one-out machinery so that a target doc is never scored by a model containing it. Compute:
   - mean bigram log-prob;
   - 10th percentile of per-pair bigram log-prob, and the fraction of pairs with log-prob below the target's global 5th percentile of pair log-probs;
   - punct-punct rate;
   - dot-capital rate;
   - OOV rate;
   - rate of capitalised words not preceded by '.' or a line start;
   - rate of immediately repeated tokens;
   - rate of function-word-to-function-word pairs, using the top 50 target words.
   Replace NaN with the clean median.

3. Classifier. Fit StandardScaler + LogisticRegression(C=1, class_weight='balanced') on clean target windows (label 0) against the synthetic negatives (label 1). Get out-of-fold scores for the clean docs with 5-fold cross-validation grouped by target doc. Set the threshold at the 98th percentile of the clean out-of-fold P(damaged). Switch the detector off if it flags fewer than 60% of the synthetic negatives at that threshold.

4. Apply to the pool. Set ok[i] = False if the existing rule fails OR P(damaged) is above the threshold, computed on the first-400-token window. Docs too short to score are kept.

Everything else stays unchanged: damaged docs are excluded from classifier positives and from dedup first-occurrences, P > 0.5, no off-domain padding, seeded shuffle. Stay inside the time limit by computing features with the same chunked numpy passes.

Add a self-gating residual-contamination audit that compares the final selected set S against target_sample and removes only a subpopulation of S that is statistically distinguishable from the target. Everything upstream stays unchanged (damage filter, classifier, dedup, junk, P > 0.5, no padding, seeded shuffle).

1. Features. After computing the chosen set S (before token counting), compute a dense feature vector on the first-400-token window after the header for every doc in S and for the 200 target docs. Reuse the existing leave-one-out scores for the target docs. Features:
   - mean bigram log-prob, OOV rate, punct-punct rate, dot-capital rate (already computed; expose them from the damage filter);
   - fraction of alphabetic tokens that are all lowercase, and fraction that are all uppercase;
   - mean whitespace-token length, and fraction of tokens longer than 15 chars;
   - distinct-token ratio, and rate of immediately repeated tokens;
   - fraction of tokens that are among the top-50 target words;
   - fraction of digit tokens;
   - fraction of single-character alphabetic tokens other than 'a', 'I' and 's';
   - rate of ' @-@ ', ' , ' and ' . ' tokens;
   - number of lines in the window, and log of the doc's char length.
   Replace NaN with the target median.

2. Two-sample classifier. Label target docs 0 and S docs 1. Fit HistGradientBoostingClassifier (max_depth=3, max_iter=100, learning_rate=0.1, class_weight='balanced', seeded) with 5-fold stratified cross-validation, and get out-of-fold P(label = 1) for every target doc and every S doc. If S has more than 6000 docs, train the folds on a seeded 6000-doc subsample of S and score the rest of S with the fold models, averaged.

3. Gate. Set tau to the 98th percentile of the target docs' out-of-fold scores. Let f be the fraction of S docs scoring above tau. If S looks like the target, f is about 2%. Apply the audit only if f >= 6% and the out-of-fold AUC is >= 0.60. Otherwise return exactly the current output.

4. Action. If the gate is open, remove the S docs scoring above tau from the chosen set, capped at the 25% of S with the highest scores. Mark them not ok and rerun the dedup pass once, so a clean duplicate of a removed doc can take its place if it passes P > 0.5 and the junk check. Then continue with token counting and the shuffle as now.

5. Record AUC, f, the number removed and W/T in a debug variable. Wrap the audit in try/except that falls back to the current behaviour. Keep it within the time limit: the features come from the existing numpy passes and the model has under 20 features.
